# Product terminology: "AI coworker", never "AI receptionist"

New Coworker is an **AI coworker**, never an "AI receptionist". Do not
introduce the phrase "AI receptionist" (any casing) anywhere: user-facing copy,
SMS/email text, SEO keywords, AI prompts, code comments, docs, PR titles, or
marketing text. All instances were removed in PR #725. Keep it that way.

```text
BAD:  your AI receptionist couldn't take a live call
GOOD: your AI coworker couldn't take a live call
```

Scope note: this bans the specific product label "AI receptionist". Standalone
"receptionist" is still allowed where it describes a role or a comparison, not
the product, e.g. the voice persona's system instruction ("You are the phone
receptionist for {business}", which tests assert on) and pricing copy comparing
cost against "a receptionist or answering service".

# Spelling: "inquiry", never "enquiry"

Write American English everywhere: user-facing copy, SMS/email text, AI
prompts, seeded AiFlow definitions, code comments, and docs. The specific ban
is the British "enquiry" family. Amy Laidlaw's follow-up cadence spent weeks
opening calls by naming the lead's source site with the British spelling,
read aloud to Arizona homeowners, because the seeded flow copy told the model
to say it (fixed 2026-08-28).

```text
BAD:  We're following up on your enquiry through Clever
GOOD: We're following up on your inquiry through Clever
```

Two mechanisms back this up, and a change to AI copy needs both:

1. `US_SPELLING_PROMPT_LINE` in
   `supabase/functions/_shared/sms_prompt_lines.ts` rides every AI
   worker/model prompt, so the models do not drift into it on unscripted
   turns. The voice bridge (`usSpellingLine`) and the document agents carry
   lockstep copies.
2. `tests/inquiry-spelling.test.ts` fails CI when the spelling appears in a
   guarded copy-first surface (prompt-line modules, preambles, message
   catalogs, email templates, and every `scripts/oneshot/*-definition.ts`).

The only unguarded places the old spelling may appear are ones that READ old
data rather than write new copy: `OLD_SITE_FALLBACK` in
`scripts/oneshot/amy-heal-parked-cadence-lead-site.ts` and the rewrite rule in
`scripts/oneshot/heal-inquiry-spelling.ts` are both matchers for rows written
before the change, and captured production transcripts under `tests/e2e/` are
replayed verbatim. Recognizing the spelling is not writing it. Anything that
produces new text belongs inside the guard.
